{"data":{"actorId":"01KSK3QAE64QAADZ4Z86DY4771","createdAt":"2026-09-04T11:51:00.027Z","eventType":"comment.status_changed","id":"33","payload":{"from":"open","reason":"Fixed on the PR branch (8f1fa760). CONFIRMED: outputpath.go gated on info.Mode()\u0026os.ModeSymlink, but Go 1.23+ reports every reparse point it does not itself decode as ModeIrregular — only SYMLINK and MOUNT_POINT stay ModeSymlink — so LX symlinks, undecodable junctions and GLOBAL_REPARSE fell through as ORDINARY components. The contained walker at agents.go:1030 already did raw-tag classification; the unconfined one did not. Now classified via unconfinedRouteAliasCandidate plus windowsRawReparseTarget, refusing an opaque alias the kernel would follow and treating inert tags as before. Fail-without-fix: 'unconfinedRouteAliasCandidate(\"windows\", ...) = false, want true'. Windows syscall path remains covered only by the Windows CI leg; cross-compilation of ./... is blocked by pre-existing cgo grammar packages, unrelated to this change.","to":"resolved"},"reviewId":"01M1NMS4WKBT2BBHFS1VH4587C","targetId":"01M1NMS4WR6FDDW6M8NJ2XF8DE","targetType":"review_comment","trailId":"01M1NC1XG2Z1PAWB1W3G44F6P7"},"event":"comment.status_changed"}
