{"data":{"actorId":"01KSK3QAE64QAADZ4Z86DY4771","createdAt":"2026-09-04T06:02:07.262Z","eventType":"comment.status_changed","id":"18","payload":{"from":"open","reason":"Fixed on the PR branch (145b2081). CONFIRMED, with two corrections to the framing. (1) The defect is the RESTART at outputpath.go:1156 ('if filepath.IsAbs(link)'), not the sticky flag — the flag condition was already conservative. (2) Terminology: '\\other\\out.json' is ROOTED but NOT fully qualified in Win32 terms (it resolves against the drive of the path being resolved); the reviewer's conclusion holds but the label does not. New linkTargetAnchorFor(goos,target) classifies 23 shapes: C:\\x, \\\\server\\share\\x, \\\\?\\C:\\x and POSIX /x are qualified and restart at their own root; '\\x' is volume-rooted and must restart at the WALKED volume (names[0]) — that was the defect, and filepath.IsAbs reports false for it; 'C:x' drive-relative is REFUSED, because the per-drive CWD is process-global and a pinned walk has no handle on it; relative forms restart at the link's parent. '\\x' takes the smaller (31) budget since it restarts at a root exactly as the qualified spellings do, per linkHopLimitFor's own doctrine that the smaller value is safe. On POSIX the classification is bit-identical to the old filepath.IsAbs behaviour, pinned by TestLinkTargetAnchorAgreesWithFilepathOnThisHost so the hand-written rules cannot drift. Windows cannot execute here: only the pure decision function is tested, and the syscall path (the restart itself, os.Root per volume spelling) is covered solely by the Windows CI leg — stated in the test doc and commit body.","to":"resolved"},"reviewId":"01M1NEVW6QW5QCX66CM7TXFVAC","targetId":"01M1NEVW6YZ17QAXRYB2PQGK53","targetType":"review_comment","trailId":"01M1NC1XG2Z1PAWB1W3G44F6P7"},"event":"comment.status_changed"}
